WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.827
This query shows AGE and direct subtraction side by side.

00:00:03.827 --> 00:00:08.057
For short hospital stays, the displayed results may look similar.

00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:12.122
The conceptual difference becomes more important across months and years.

00:00:12.122 --> 00:00:18.972
Always choose the function based on the meaning you want to communicate rather than because two examples

00:00:18.972 --> 00:00:20.907
happen to produce similar text.

00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:23.887
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:29.632
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:29.632 --> 00:00:31.827
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:36.767
In a real setting such as an operations team aligning timestamps across locations,

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:42.752
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:42.752 --> 00:00:44.222
before discussing syntax.

00:00:44.222 --> 00:00:50.000
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
